main='''<main id="main"><div class="atlas-breadcrumb" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/">Overview</a><span aria-hidden="true">/</span><a href="/evidence/">Evidence</a><span aria-hidden="true">/</span><span aria-current="page">Sampled complex monitor</span></div><div class="page-intro"><div class="eyebrow">22 October 2026 Chicago · Sampled witness 0.1</div><h1>A sampled witness.<br>Explicit timing and contamination costs.</h1><p class="lede">An exact sampled-Gram bound connects complex monitor values to a conditional leakage certificate. Digital RMS arithmetic is verified on serialized synthetic samples.</p></div><aside class="callout"><strong>Exact conditional design · Synthetic digital check · Physical promotion 0</strong><p>No empirical monitor data or calibration is admitted. Independent review pending. Source completeness and the tightened 10 µV outside-model cap remain unverified; EXIT-03 and the circuit candidate stay HOLD.</p></aside><div class="actions"><a class="button secondary" href="/sources/sampled-monitor-v0.1/THEOREM.md">Read theorem and proof ↗</a><a class="text-link" href="/sources/sampled-monitor-v0.1/CERTIFICATE.json">Exact certificate</a></div><section class="chapter"><div class="chapter-label">01</div><div><h2>Sample timing enters the Gram bound.</h2><p>The proposed independent monitor has 1024 complex samples on a nominal one-second cyclic grid and finite modes 100–199 Hz. The first-to-last nominal sample span is 1023/1024 seconds.</p><p>Per-mode frequency error ≤5 mHz, affine clock error ≤10 ppb and per-sample residual ≤10 ns give a sampled-Gram eigenvalue lower bound above 0.9096. Epoch and common frequency shifts leave that bound invariant but do not certify absolute phase.</p><table><tbody><tr><th>Digital RMS / total monitor error</th><td>≤55 µV / ≤20 µV</td></tr><tr><th>Independent outside-model variation</th><td>≤10 µV</td></tr><tr><th>Conditional tail variation</th><td>≤0.90675 mV</td></tr><tr><th>Propagated full-state error / phase</th><td>&lt;0.008367 V / &lt;0.016903 rad</td></tr></tbody></table></div></section><section class="chapter"><div class="chapter-label">02</div><div><h2>Raw-monitor contamination is charged directly.</h2><p>The outside-model contribution appears inside the sample-norm bound and in the full tail reference. With the previous 200 µV outside cap, this direct route’s sufficient bound would miss the target. The new 10 µV cap is a prospective stronger requirement, not an observed result.</p><p>The independent-monitor error allocation is 10 µV acquisition/quantization, 5 µV quadrature calibration, 3 µV baseline and 2 µV numerical error. Applicable full-path calibration remains necessary; these requirements are separate from the original acquisition budget.</p><a class="text-link" href="/sources/sampled-monitor-v0.1/REPORT.md">Read budgets and limits →</a></div></section><section class="chapter"><div class="chapter-label">03</div><div><h2>Exact digital arithmetic cannot exclude unknown aliases.</h2><p>The synthetic fixture is stored as binary64 hex words. Rational mean-squared norm and an integer ceiling square root verify its digital RMS; five corrupted/non-fixture controls return HOLD. No physical source accuracy is inferred.</p><p>Modes at 100 and 1124 Hz alias exactly on the nominal grid. Opposite source coefficients can therefore vanish in the sampled record while their spectral variation remains large. Independent source completeness and outside-model evidence are indispensable.</p><p>Next bind real monitor timestamps, I/Q calibration, source preparation, outside-model evidence and computation hashes into a reviewable receipt contract.</p><a class="text-link" href="/sources/sampled-monitor-v0.1/SYNTHETIC_SAMPLES.json">Synthetic samples only ↓</a><a class="text-link" href="/sources/sampled-monitor-v0.1/certify.py">Certificate and fixture replay ↓</a><a class="text-link" href="/evidence/gram-witness/">Prior continuous-time witness →</a></div></section></main>'''
